# A line wider than the window. Milestone one cut it and let the cursor stop
# at the edge; ADR-0397 scrolls it, which is `top`'s rule on the other axis --
# where the window sits is a property of the last drawing, so `left` lives
# beside `top` in the document and `EditRender` is what moves it.
#
# Four claims:
#
#   - End scrolls the window so the cursor is on it, and what is drawn is the
#     *last* twelve columns rather than the first;
#   - Home scrolls back, and the first twelve return;
#   - the `~` filler stays at column one however far the window has scrolled.
#     It says *past the end of the document* and is not text at a column, so
#     scrolling it away would make a scrolled window look like a longer
#     document;
#   - a wide character straddling the left edge is **not half-drawn** -- the
#     last frame starts with a blank where half of one would have been,
#     because a terminal handed the first half of a wide character does
#     not draw half of it;
#   - and it scrolls by **column** and not by byte, which the second line is
#     for: fifteen wide characters are thirty columns, so the window holding
#     twelve of them holds six characters. A byte-wise scroll would put the
#     window inside a character and draw half of one.
#
# The status line goes on counting bytes throughout -- 1:27 for the end of the
# Latin line and 1:46 for the end of the Japanese one -- which is ADR-0395's
# decision and is what makes Ctrl-B land where `pascalc` says.
name wide.pas
size 7 12
push abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz
push 日本語を編集するときの横スクロール
draw
esc F
draw
esc H
draw
esc B
esc F
draw
esc D
esc D
esc D
draw
